WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:22.679
Today’s protagonist is 
the

00:00:22.679 --> 00:00:29.210
Generalisimo Francisco Franco, who ruled Spain
from 1939 to 1975 with the title of ‘Caudillo’,

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:31.179
leading one of the longest running dictatorships
in Europe.

00:00:31.179 --> 00:00:35.320
He is best remembered as the leader of the
Nationalist faction, the military insurgency

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:39.020
which opposed the Republican government in
the Spanish Civil War.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:42.670
But how did he rise through the ranks and
seized control of a movement that he had not

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:43.670
initiated?

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:48.690
How did he rule Spain after the war and how
did he orchestrate the transition back to

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:49.690
the Monarchy?

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:54.670
We are going to cover these and other aspects
of Franco’s life in today’s Biographics.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:59.390
Before we start, a disclaimer: an extensive
account of Francisco Franco’s life implies

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:03.969
telling the History of a complex conflict
and of a whole nation for more than 40 years.

00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:06.520
So, we are going to skip some chunks in today’s
video.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:11.720
If you think we have missed an important event,
post your suggestions in the comments – make

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:17.110
sure that you name the protagonist, or protagonists
of these events, and we may consider future

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:18.350
videos about them.

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:20.400
Bueno, ya he charlado demasiado.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.860
Empecemos con la historia del Caudillo!

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.250
The Small Cadet
Francisco Franco Bahamonde was born on the

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:30.270
4th of December 1892, in the town of El Ferrol,
North Western Spain.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:34.920
He had two brothers and one sister, born into
a family that for generations had served the

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.520
once glorious Spanish Navy.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:41.210
Sister Pilar described him as a very quiet
and serious boy, physically very thin.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:45.450
In his childhood Francisco had two obsessions:
his studies and his mother, with whom he had

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:46.970
a very tight relationship.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:51.550
Francisco’s mother Maria del Pilar was an
austere and devout woman, who imbued Francisco

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:56.880
with a deep faith in religion and in the importance
of the Church in Spanish society.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:02.130
His father was more interested in alcohol
and extra-marital affairs, a life-style which

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:05.900
Francisco perceived as debauched and actively
avoided throughout his life.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:10.590
In 1898 the once mighty Spanish Empire was
reduced to a handful of colonies following

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:16.560
the defeat in the Hispano-American war, which
cost them Cuba, Puerto Rico and the Philippines.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.910
The remnants of the defeated Spanish fleet
returned to El Ferrol, marking a period of

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:24.320
mourning for the whole community and especially
for the six-year-old.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:28.070
This may have instilled in him a desire to
restore the glory of the Empire.

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:32.120
The other effect of the destruction of the
fleet was a decline in available spots for

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:34.320
the Naval Academy for several years.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.950
The Navy would have been Francisco’s natural
destination, but he had to settle for an Army

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:39.950
career instead.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:44.060
In 1906, aged 14, Francisco enlisted in the
Toledo Army academy.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:49.260
The new cadet was so small, his voice so shrill
that he quickly became victim of the academy’s

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:50.260
bullies.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:54.000
His frame was so frail that the instructors
had to modify his training rifle to make it

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.030
lighter to carry.

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:59.600
Francisco made up for his physical shortcomings
with determination and hard work, although

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.460
he did not display particular leadership qualities.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:07.990
He graduated in 1910 with a modest 251st place
out of 312 cadets.

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:12.470
The Spanish military was saturated with officers
at that time and opportunities for career

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:14.060
progression were scarce.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:19.010
In 1912 Francisco took the calculated move
of volunteering for a post in Spanish Morocco,

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:22.480
the last colony, as the only chance to progress
in ranks.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.720
His experiences in Africa would be key in
shaping his later life.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.210
He later declared:
“Without Africa, I could not explain who

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:37.110
I am”
El Novio de la Muerte

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:41.450
The Kingdom of Spain was engaged in a brutal
colonial war against the rebel tribes of the

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:42.569
Rif mountain range.

00:03:42.569 --> 00:03:48.310
In this conflict Lieutenant Franco displayed
qualities so far kept hidden: tenacity and

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:49.310
physical courage.

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:53.560
Lieutenant Franco was in charge of a company
of Moroccan colonial troops, loyal to the

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:54.560
Spanish crown.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.290
He was able to maintain a ruthless discipline
amongst his soldiers, even when under heavy

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:00.420
enemy fire.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:04.390
On one occasion one of his troops refused
to eat his rations and threw them at another

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:05.390
officer.

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:07.710
Franco had him executed by firing squad.

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:12.930
It is alleged that he applied this ruthlessness
also against civilians, using terror tactics

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:14.160
to quell the rebellion.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.329
The officers of the Spanish army in Africa
at that time were nicknamed ‘Novios de la

00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:22.960
Muerte’, which can be translated as ‘the
grooms (or boyfriends) of Lady Death’.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:26.410
This was because of the high casualty rates
amongst their ranks.

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:30.970
Franco apparently disregarded the fear of
death, often leading from the front almost

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:33.730
suicidal attacks against entrenched rebels.

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:38.560
In 1917 Franco had already been promoted Major
and was ordered back to Spain to break a strike

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.910
of coal miners in the northern region of Asturias.

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:46.730
During his stay in Oviedo, the capital of
the region, he got engaged with Carmen Polo,

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:49.560
despite opposition from her family of wealthy
merchants.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:54.850
Back to Morocco in 1918, on the 28th of June
he was shot in the stomach, an almost certain

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:56.600
appointment with Lady Death.

00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:00.870
While bleeding out on a stretcher he called
out to a doctor, but he was refused treatment.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:03.300
According to the doctor, it would have been
futile.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:07.210
Franco then grabbed a rifle and pointed it
at the doctor, threatening to shoot him if

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:08.260
he was not treated.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:11.550
The medic changed his mind and had him evacuated
to the field hospital.

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:13.820
Against all odds, he fully recovered.

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:16.970
The native troops followed him with blind
loyalty afterwards.

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:22.150
In October of 1920 Franco was second-in-command
of the Tercio de Extranjeros, the Spanish

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:26.600
Foreign Legion recently founded by Lieutenant-Colonel
Millan Astray.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.060
Millan introduced as such the credo of the
Tercio:

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:31.480
“Death in combat is the greatest honour.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:32.870
You die only once.

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:36.220
Death arrives without pain and is not so terrible
as it seems.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:42.010
The most horrible thing is to live as a coward”
The Tercio became known for its effectiveness,

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:43.890
courage and brutality.

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:47.050
One of Franco’s soldiers, described as such
their operations:

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:50.490
“When it attacked, the Tercio knew no limits
to its vengeance.

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:56.520
When it left a village, nothing remained but
fires and the corpses of men, women and children”

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:01.420
In 1923 Franco became commander of the Tercio,
shining for his strategic and administrative

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:02.420
ability.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:07.280
On the 23rd of October he married Carmen in
Oviedo, with a representative of King Alfonso

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:09.110
XIII present at the wedding.

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:12.380
They would have only one daughter, Carmencita,
born the following year.

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:16.740
After 14 years of service in Morocco, the
once mediocre Lieutenant had progressed so

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:19.990
quickly that he returned to Spain a Brigadier
General.

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:23.479
Aged 33, he was the youngest General in Europe
since Napoleon.

00:06:23.479 --> 00:06:28.110
He had also become something of a celebrity,
frequenting elite circles and even starring

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:31.650
as himself in the movie ‘La Malcasada’
of 1926.

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:36.090
This curious film, dealing with a real-life
divorce case, featured many intellectuals,

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:38.210
politicians and military leaders of the time.

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:42.240
Starring alongside Franco can be seen his
old commander Millan Astray, who would later

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:45.120
become Nationalist propaganda chief during
the Civil War.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:50.820
And even Miguel Primo de Rivera, who was ruling
Spain as a military dictator from 1923 with

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:52.020
the blessing of the King.

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:56.810
Franco’s celebrity status and Carmen’s
connections gave him access to the elitist

00:06:56.810 --> 00:06:59.669
world, but he did not enjoy their pleasures.

00:06:59.669 --> 00:07:04.240
He preferred to relish the traditional and
austere values he had learned from his mother,

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:08.140
which he imparted to his cadets at the new
military academy in Zaragoza.

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:12.610
But despite his best efforts for a return
to tradition, Spanish society was changing

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:14.800
around the young General.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:23.020
End of an era
From the bottom up, Spanish society was shaken

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:27.650
by the rise of leftist movements opposing
the traditional pillars of the Monarchy, the

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:30.639
Catholic Church and owners of large land estates.

00:07:30.639 --> 00:07:35.229
At the top, Primo de Rivera’s dictatorship
was failing due to his ill-conceived economic

00:07:35.229 --> 00:07:40.669
policies resulting in high taxation, unsustainable
public debt and raising inflation.

00:07:40.669 --> 00:07:42.900
He was forced to resign in January 1930.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:48.400
In 1931 Alfonso XIII agreed to democratic
elections, which saw the victory of the left.

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:50.110
In April, the King abdicated.

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:54.330
The Spanish Republic was founded and a new
constitution ratified.

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:58.230
According to Franco’s niece, this was a
personal catastrophe for him, as he did not

00:07:58.230 --> 00:08:01.520
believe in democracy nor in a multi-party
system.

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:07.260
But the new Government was a fragile one and
in November 1933 it was replaced by a conservative

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:11.180
coalition, who appointed General Franco as
Commander in chief of the Army.

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:15.039
The left continued to cause unrest, though:
in 1934 it was launched another massive strike

00:08:15.039 --> 00:08:17.620
of coal miners in Asturias.

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:19.870
Franco was tasked with restoring order in
the area.

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:22.870
He commanded the Tercio to take charge of
the strikers.

00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:26.990
2000 protesters were killed or wounded, many
more deported to Morocco.

00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:31.440
But his actions had the unintended consequence
of rallying the left wing parties into a strong

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:34.039
coalition, called ‘Frente Popular’ or
‘People’s Front’.

00:08:34.039 --> 00:08:36.679
In February 1936 new elections were convened.

00:08:36.679 --> 00:08:39.740
This time it was the leftist People’s Front
to secure a victory.

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:44.110
The new government ensured that conservative
generals were not close to the seat of power.

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:48.649
Franco was dispatched to Tenerife in the Canary
Islands, 2000 km from Madrid.

00:08:48.649 --> 00:08:52.970
The Government kept close tabs on him, by
wiretapping his phone and checking his mail.

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:57.180
In the meanwhile, unrest and violence were
spiralling in the country.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:01.980
The supporters of the leftist government soon
gave way to excesses, especially against Catholic

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:02.980
institutions.

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:05.290
Churches were being burned and priests murdered.

00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:07.889
Behind the scenes, a group of Generals was
plotting a ‘golpe militar’ or military

00:09:07.889 --> 00:09:08.889
coup.

00:09:08.889 --> 00:09:12.450
Franco did not get involved at this stage,
coldly calculating the risks involved.

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:16.129
The leaders of the plot were General Sanjurjo
and General Mola.

00:09:16.129 --> 00:09:21.350
Mola, the chief planner of the coup, had a
scheme to involve Franco in the ‘Bando Nacional’,

00:09:21.350 --> 00:09:22.730
or Nationalist Forces.

00:09:22.730 --> 00:09:27.089
The plan involved dispatching a plane from
London to the Canaries, to then smuggle Franco

00:09:27.089 --> 00:09:31.209
into Morocco, where he would lead the Tercio
to join the insurrection.

00:09:31.209 --> 00:09:34.790
While on the 11th of July the plane was flying
to Gran Canaria, Franco sent an encrypted

00:09:34.790 --> 00:09:37.759
message to Mola, refusing to participate in
the coup.

00:09:37.759 --> 00:09:42.730
But on the 13th, right wing politician José
Calvo Sotelo was murdered by the Republican

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:48.449
police, which gave to the insurgents the motivation
to proceed with their plans at all costs.

00:09:48.449 --> 00:09:50.220
Franco was finally convinced.

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:54.959
On the 19th of July 1936 he travelled in civilian
disguise to the Moroccan colony.

00:09:54.959 --> 00:09:59.259
The African Army, numbering 30,000, swore
loyalty to Franco and to his cause.

00:09:59.259 --> 00:10:04.679
But how could he ferry such an army to Spain,
when the Navy was loyal to the Republic and

00:10:04.679 --> 00:10:06.939
was patrolling the straits of Gibraltar?

00:10:06.939 --> 00:10:10.999
This is when he secured help from Fascist
Italy and Nazi Germany.

00:10:10.999 --> 00:10:15.110
The Italian and German air forces came to
help Franco: by organising 800 flights they

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:18.009
managed to ferry his forces to mainland Spain.

00:10:18.009 --> 00:10:21.619
The hopes of the insurgents for a quick resolution
of the coup did not materialise.

00:10:21.619 --> 00:10:26.619
What had just started was a long and bloody
conflict, the Spanish Civil War.

00:10:26.619 --> 00:10:30.420
The insurgents also had a leadership problem:
their commander General Sanjurjo had died

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:35.389
in a plane crash on the 20th of July, being
replaced by military Junta only formally led

00:10:35.389 --> 00:10:37.480
by the elder General Cabanellas.

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:41.649
In the meanwhile, Franco had reached Seville
and quickly led his army northwards, facing

00:10:41.649 --> 00:10:43.369
only the resistance of civilians.

00:10:43.369 --> 00:10:46.709
His African Army soon reached Badajoz, near
the Portuguese border.

00:10:46.709 --> 00:10:50.220
After conquering the city, Franco embarked
in a political cleansing of the area.

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:54.430
As witnessed by Portuguese journalist Mario
Neves, hundreds of prisoners were rounded

00:10:54.430 --> 00:10:58.379
up in the local bull-fighting ring and executed
by firing squad.

00:10:58.379 --> 00:11:02.649
Franco continued his march towards Madrid,
but in September he ordered a diversion to

00:11:02.649 --> 00:11:04.269
Toledo, south of the capital.

00:11:04.269 --> 00:11:09.040
In Toledo a small garrison of Nationalists
was besieged by the Republican army inside

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:13.120
the Alcazar, the castle which housed the very
military academy in which young Francisco

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:14.120
had graduated.

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:18.079
Franco’s army defeated the Republicans,
but he only joined them the following day,

00:11:18.079 --> 00:11:21.239
27th of September, in full view of the news
cameras.

00:11:21.239 --> 00:11:25.459
He had realised that this was more of a PR
event than a simple military victory.

00:11:25.459 --> 00:11:30.740
Toledo was not only home to the Military Academy,
it was also the religious capital of Spain.

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:35.839
By appearing as the liberator of Toledo Franco
had portrayed himself as the defender of Spanish

00:11:35.839 --> 00:12:27.649
traditional institutions.

00:12:27.649 --> 00:12:59.829
A coup within a coup
On

00:12:59.829 --> 00:13:10.679
the

00:13:10.679 --> 00:13:13.240
same evening, Franco travelled to the city
of Caceres.

00:13:13.240 --> 00:13:18.209
Here, General Millan Astray and General Yague
announced that now the whole Nationalist Army

00:13:18.209 --> 00:13:20.689
was under the sole command of General Franco.

00:13:20.689 --> 00:13:25.199
So far Francisco had been leading only the
African Army in the south, while General Mola

00:13:25.199 --> 00:13:26.600
was in command in the North.

00:13:26.600 --> 00:13:31.389
All the generals, except for the old Cabanellas,
thought that a unified leadership would improve

00:13:31.389 --> 00:13:32.540
their war efforts.

00:13:32.540 --> 00:13:36.050
Franco was one of the candidates, but he was
not sure about taking the mantle.

00:13:36.050 --> 00:13:40.389
It was thanks to the scheming of his own brother
Nicolás that he eventually accepted.

00:13:40.389 --> 00:13:44.630
Nicolás first pressured Yague into convincing
Francisco to lead all armies.

00:13:44.630 --> 00:13:48.889
Yague issued a vague threat to Franco: if
he didn’t accept the role, the Junta would

00:13:48.889 --> 00:13:50.410
appoint somebody else.

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:55.540
Nicolás also orchestrated a meeting of eight
leading Generals, including Franco, in Salamanca,

00:13:55.540 --> 00:13:57.329
on the 28th of September 1936.

00:13:57.329 --> 00:14:02.569
The purpose of the meeting was whether to
concentrate on one single person both military

00:14:02.569 --> 00:14:06.290
and political leadership of the Spanish State
during the Civil War.

00:14:06.290 --> 00:14:09.949
Except for two Generals, everybody voted for
Franco to be the leader of the Spanish State,

00:14:09.949 --> 00:14:13.829
a term which indicated the Nationalist controlled
territory.

00:14:13.829 --> 00:14:18.290
Franco now had become the Generalisimo of
all armed forces and head of the Government.

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:21.759
He took on the title of Caudillo, an ancient
style for ‘Leader’.

00:14:21.759 --> 00:14:24.769
In theory, he only had a mandate for the duration
of the war.

00:14:24.769 --> 00:14:28.850
But Nicolás cleverly redacted the Official
Bulletin bearing the decision, so that this

00:14:28.850 --> 00:14:30.420
time limitation was omitted.

00:14:30.420 --> 00:14:34.759
The Franco brothers had outsmarted the other
generals, a real coup within a coup, which

00:14:34.759 --> 00:14:42.189
ensured total power for life to the once reluctant
insurgent.

00:14:42.189 --> 00:14:47.799
Continuing the War
By October 1936 Nationalist forces were in

00:14:47.799 --> 00:14:52.370
control of the roughly one third of Spanish
territory, mainly in the West and North of

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:53.370
the Country.

00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:58.429
But Madrid was still out of Franco’s reach,
despite intensive air and artillery bombings.

00:14:58.429 --> 00:15:01.470
At this time, the Republican government secured
support from Stalin.

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:05.339
Now, every side of the conflict could rely
on help from powerful dictatorships.

00:15:05.339 --> 00:15:09.629
This War would not spare civilians, it was
a total war in which many of the horrific

00:15:09.629 --> 00:15:12.040
tactics of WWII would be tested.

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:15.579
Atrocities were perpetrated on a massive scale,
on both sides.

00:15:15.579 --> 00:15:19.899
Franco and the other Generals enacted a policy
of purging captured cities, following the

00:15:19.899 --> 00:15:21.189
example of Badajoz.

00:15:21.189 --> 00:15:25.500
The Republicans were also active in executing
members of the clergy, Nationalist prisoners,

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:30.359
Nationalist sympathisers and even themselves,
as a result of infighting among disparate

00:15:30.359 --> 00:15:32.949
factions – most notably in Catalunya.

00:15:32.949 --> 00:15:37.569
It is estimated that by the end of the war
the Nationalists had executed 75,000 people,

00:15:37.569 --> 00:15:40.300
compared to 55,000 murdered by Republicans.

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:46.049
In February 1937, Franco attempted to take
Madrid by frontal assault, in the Battle of

00:15:46.049 --> 00:15:50.889
the Jarama river, but was stopped by the International
Brigades of foreign volunteers.

00:15:50.889 --> 00:15:56.019
It is interesting to note that 5,000 Germans
and 3,000 Italians fought against Franco,

00:15:56.019 --> 00:15:58.819
joining the International Brigades in support
of the Republic.

00:15:58.819 --> 00:16:01.769
These brigades were 60,000 strong at their
peak.

00:16:01.769 --> 00:16:06.230
In March Franco approached the capital from
North East, capturing Guadalajara first.

00:16:06.230 --> 00:16:11.579
An Italian Corps took the city and then moved
quickly to Madrid, but was repelled by a Republican

00:16:11.579 --> 00:16:13.749
and Soviet tank counterattack.

00:16:13.749 --> 00:16:17.660
Operations progressed for the Nationalists
in the north: In August 1937 they captured

00:16:17.660 --> 00:16:23.250
the Northern stronghold of Santander with
help from Italian regular army and Blackshirts.

00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:25.910
This helped consolidate their territories
in the North.

00:16:25.910 --> 00:16:29.829
In the meanwhile, the German Condor Legion
and the Fascist Air Force had been perpetrating

00:16:29.829 --> 00:16:34.860
the first large carpet bombings of civilian
targets over Durango, Barcelona, Madrid and

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:39.059
most notably, Guernica, on the 26th of April
1937.

00:16:39.059 --> 00:16:43.999
But all in all, despite decisive support from
his allies, Franco’s progress was slow.

00:16:43.999 --> 00:16:48.679
German leadership was frustrated by this and
suspected it was a calculated move on his

00:16:48.679 --> 00:16:53.499
side: Franco’s strategy was to firmly consolidate
his own political power before advancing on

00:16:53.499 --> 00:16:54.499
new territories.

00:16:54.499 --> 00:16:58.860
The situation for the Republicans became dire
in the 2nd half of 1938.

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:03.749
In July they engaged the Nationalists in a
protracted battle on the river Ebro, in the

00:17:03.749 --> 00:17:04.749
North East.

00:17:04.749 --> 00:17:10.660
It lasted 113 days and claimed more than 13,000
casualties on both sides.

00:17:10.660 --> 00:17:15.150
The Nationalists, 600,000 strong, could take
the hit, the Republicans, less so.

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:19.880
In September of 1938 the International Brigades
withdrew from Spain, while Mussolini sent

00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:22.190
even more aircraft and artillery to Franco.

00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:25.880
On the 26th of January 1939, Barcelona fell
to the Nationalists.

00:17:25.880 --> 00:17:29.520
The President of the Republic, Manuel Azaña,
fled to France.

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:33.900
On the 27th of February, British Prime Minister
Neville Chamberlain was the first European

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:37.540
democratic leader to recognize Franco’s
Government as legitimate.

00:17:37.540 --> 00:17:43.220
On the same day Azaña resigned and by the
1st of April all Republican forces had capitulated.

00:17:43.220 --> 00:17:44.920
It was the end of the war.

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:48.840
Half a million people had died, 2% of the
total Spanish population.

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:51.000
But the death toll would not stop there.

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:55.420
As a sole ruler, dictator or Caudillo, of
the Spanish State, Francisco Franco could

00:17:55.420 --> 00:17:56.660
not tolerate dissent.

00:17:56.660 --> 00:18:00.840
After the war had ended, he continued his
policy of purging the country from enemies

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:05.060
of his regime via executions ruled by military
tribunals.

00:18:05.060 --> 00:18:10.270
Sources differ, but it is alleged that between
30,000 and 200,000 dissidents were executed

00:18:10.270 --> 00:18:12.710
by Franco’s regime.

00:18:12.710 --> 00:18:19.470
The Caudillo and The Fuehrer
Following the beginning of WWII in Europe

00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:23.430
there was a debate as to whether Spain should
join the Axis powers in their fight.

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:27.810
On the 23rd of October 1940 Franco and Hitler
met in Hendaye, in Southern France.

00:18:27.810 --> 00:18:31.563
Franco owed much to the Germans, but during
that meeting he did not commit to an active

00:18:31.563 --> 00:18:32.980
participation in the war.

00:18:32.980 --> 00:18:37.380
This decision was considered a wise one, and
defended by Francoist supporters over time.

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:42.200
The Spanish military and industrial capabilities
had been exhausted by the Civil War, and they

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:43.782
were not in shape of joining another conflict.

00:18:43.782 --> 00:18:45.522
However, a member of Von Ribbentrop’s staff
…

00:18:45.522 --> 00:18:46.522
[Editing suggestion: show a photo of Joachim
Von Ribbentrop with the caption ‘Joachim

00:18:46.522 --> 00:18:47.522
Von Ribbentrop – German Minister for Foreign
Affairs’]

00:18:47.522 --> 00:18:50.820
… Interviewed by the BBC reported that there
had been a previous meeting in Berlin.

00:18:50.820 --> 00:18:54.820
Ribbentrop had met with Franco’s foreign
affairs minister, Ramón Serrano Suñer.

00:18:54.820 --> 00:18:59.230
The minister proposed to join the Axis, in
exchange for a large chunk of North African

00:18:59.230 --> 00:19:03.990
territory to rebuild the Spanish Empire, but
at that time the Germans had not been impressed

00:19:03.990 --> 00:19:04.990
by the offer.

00:19:04.990 --> 00:19:08.580
At the Hendaye meeting, again Franco offered
his participation in the war, in exchange

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:11.610
for all of French Morocco, Algeria and Tunisia.

00:19:11.610 --> 00:19:15.570
Hitler could not accept these demands and
the negotiations did not progress.

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:17.130
The Fuehrer was furious.

00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:21.000
This is when he famously said that he’d
rather have four teeth pulled off than having

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:22.190
another talk with Franco.

00:19:22.190 --> 00:19:26.820
Later in the war Franco was astute enough
to maintain an equidistant neutrality with

00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:28.680
both Axis and Allies.

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:33.250
For example, on the one hand he allowed allied
pilots downed over Spanish territory to re-join

00:19:33.250 --> 00:19:36.130
their countries, and he never made a move
on Gibraltar.

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:40.280
On the other hand, he allowed Italian Navy
frogmen to launch raids on Gibraltar from

00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:41.380
Spanish ports.

00:19:41.380 --> 00:19:46.420
By 1941, he was maintaining good trade relations
with the US and the UK, but in that summer

00:19:46.420 --> 00:19:51.890
he allowed a contingent of 47,000 volunteers
to join Operation Barbarossa against the Soviet

00:19:51.890 --> 00:19:52.890
Union.

00:19:52.890 --> 00:19:57.250
This contingent, known as ‘La Division Azúl’,
or Blue Division, was recalled in October

00:19:57.250 --> 00:20:01.540
of 1943, when it was clear that the war on
the Eastern front was a lost cause for the

00:20:01.540 --> 00:20:02.540
Axis.

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:06.540
This marked Franco’s opportunistic shift
towards the Allies in the latter part of the

00:20:06.540 --> 00:20:08.150
War.

00:20:08.150 --> 00:20:14.480
A Gradual Transition
After the war Franco came under considerable

00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:16.280
pressure to restore the monarchy.

00:20:16.280 --> 00:20:20.300
In 1947 Franco announced a referendum to let
the Spanish people decide.

00:20:20.300 --> 00:20:24.610
The vote confirmed a preference for the monarchy,
however the ever-astute Franco, while accepting

00:20:24.610 --> 00:20:28.780
the decision, declared himself as lifetime
regent, with the privilege of appointing a

00:20:28.780 --> 00:20:32.030
further regent, or a King, before his death.

00:20:32.030 --> 00:20:36.250
Following the death of Alfonso XIII in 1941,
the natural heir to the throne was his third

00:20:36.250 --> 00:20:39.150
son Don Juan of Bourbon, Count of Barcelona.

00:20:39.150 --> 00:20:42.230
Don Juan’s eldest brothers had both renounced
the succession.

00:20:42.230 --> 00:20:46.220
Franco however was under pressure from two
components of his power base: the Falangist

00:20:46.220 --> 00:20:48.000
and the Carlist movements.

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:52.720
The Falangists were anti-monarchic, while
the Carlist supported another branch of the

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:53.720
Bourbons.

00:20:53.720 --> 00:20:58.850
Franco managed to placate both by selecting
as future king the son of Don Juan, named

00:20:58.850 --> 00:20:59.850
Juan Carlos.

00:20:59.850 --> 00:21:04.690
Reluctantly, Don Juan agreed and in 1949 the
ten-year-old Juan Carlos travelled to Madrid

00:21:04.690 --> 00:21:08.070
to begin his studies and training as future
Monarch.

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:11.590
While planning for his succession, Franco
had to think about rebuilding his country,

00:21:11.590 --> 00:21:14.870
weakened by the war and internationally isolated.

00:21:14.870 --> 00:21:19.620
International community saw with suspicion
his regime, a remnant of fascist dictatorships

00:21:19.620 --> 00:21:20.730
from before the war.

00:21:20.730 --> 00:21:25.150
In fact, Franco’s regime did offer sanctuary
to many former Fascists and Nazis - a prime

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:30.880
example being former SS Commando Otto Skorzeny,
managing a thriving business in Madrid.

00:21:30.880 --> 00:21:34.930
Franco embarked on a project to reconcile
the opposing factions of the Civil War: the

00:21:34.930 --> 00:21:36.430
‘Valle de los Caidos’, or Valley of the
Fallen.

00:21:36.430 --> 00:21:40.150
This is a monumental shrine, built over 20
years, not far from Madrid.

00:21:40.150 --> 00:21:44.620
It is topped by a gigantic cross, taller than
the Eiffel Tower and it houses the remains

00:21:44.620 --> 00:21:47.780
of 50,000 Nationalist and Republican soldiers.

00:21:47.780 --> 00:21:51.930
Franco’s intention was to commemorate the
fallen, heal the rift between enemies and

00:21:51.930 --> 00:21:55.140
lay a promise not to engage in further civil
conflict.

00:21:55.140 --> 00:21:59.610
The intention was good, although some sources
claim that it was built thanks to forced labour

00:21:59.610 --> 00:22:01.110
of Republican prisoners.

00:22:01.110 --> 00:22:04.950
Looking externally, Franco sought to re-establish
ties with the West.

00:22:04.950 --> 00:22:08.680
His strong anti-Communism stance made him
especially popular with the United States,

00:22:08.680 --> 00:22:12.250
and they backed Spain’s entry into the United
Nations in 1950.

00:22:12.250 --> 00:22:17.250
The relationship with the US was cemented
in 1953: Franco and President Eisenhower signed

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:21.900
an agreement that enabled the United States
to establish four military bases in Spain.

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:24.630
In return, Spain was allowed to join NATO.

00:22:24.630 --> 00:22:29.140
In the same year, Franco got recognition by
the smallest – but by no means powerless

00:22:29.140 --> 00:22:31.180
– State in the world: the Vatican.

00:22:31.180 --> 00:22:36.060
Pope Pius XII granted Franco the right to
appoint Spanish bishops from a list of several

00:22:36.060 --> 00:22:38.410
candidates proposed by the Holy See.

00:22:38.410 --> 00:22:42.230
In 1956 Morocco gained independence, after
being a French protectorate.

00:22:42.230 --> 00:22:46.210
The Moroccan government demanded authority
also over the Sidi-Ifni territory, one of

00:22:46.210 --> 00:22:49.950
the last remnants of Spanish possessions in
North Africa.

00:22:49.950 --> 00:22:55.320
Franco denied this concession, as ever willing
to preserve at least a slice of the Empire.

00:22:55.320 --> 00:23:00.050
Morocco reacted by creating a Moroccan Liberation
Army, which engaged the Spanish garrison in

00:23:00.050 --> 00:23:04.600
a series of skirmishes, until the Generalisimo
ordered the intervention of paratroopers from

00:23:04.600 --> 00:23:05.600
the motherland.

00:23:05.600 --> 00:23:09.360
The war – known as the ‘Spanish forgotten
war’ – dragged on into 1958, with even

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:12.390
the French Army and Air Force joining Franco
in February.

00:23:12.390 --> 00:23:16.470
By April the Moroccans had been defeated,
but Spain was pressured by the UN and the

00:23:16.470 --> 00:23:18.830
US to hand back the region of Tarfaya.

00:23:18.830 --> 00:23:23.670
Spain retained control of Sidi-Ifni until
1969, when it returned the former colony to

00:23:23.670 --> 00:23:25.890
Morocco following a UN resolution.

00:23:25.890 --> 00:23:31.390
In 1963 Franco had launched a program to soften
his totalitarian regime, in a bid to further

00:23:31.390 --> 00:23:34.890
improve relationships with the West, and the
US in particular.

00:23:34.890 --> 00:23:39.550
He terminated the activities of military tribunals
and had them replaced with civilian courts.

00:23:39.550 --> 00:23:44.390
He also shut down the special courts created
to trial Communists and Freemasons.

00:23:44.390 --> 00:23:49.210
Other reforms included laws supporting a partial
freedom of the press and freedom of worship.

00:23:49.210 --> 00:23:54.020
Until then, the regime had actively suppressed
the activities of all opposition: left wing

00:23:54.020 --> 00:23:58.940
parties, trade unions and regional separatists,
who were very vocal especially in the Basque

00:23:58.940 --> 00:24:00.580
country and Catalunya.

00:24:00.580 --> 00:24:05.220
Franco’s reforms and ouvertures to the West
had succeeded in stabilising the country and

00:24:05.220 --> 00:24:06.220
the economy.

00:24:06.220 --> 00:24:10.080
Repression of dissenters was toned down, but
the Generalisimo still maintained a tough

00:24:10.080 --> 00:24:11.780
stance against separatism.

00:24:11.780 --> 00:24:16.230
In August of 1965 President Johnson asked
for Spain’s participation in the Vietnam

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:17.230
War.

00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:21.341
The Caudillo rejected the invitation, logically
wary of engaging in guerrilla warfare in a

00:24:21.341 --> 00:24:22.350
far-away jungle.

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:25.210
But he did order for a team of field medics
to join the war.

00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:30.130
Meanwhile, Franco, his advisor Luis Carrero
Blanco and prince Juan Carlos, were planning

00:24:30.130 --> 00:24:31.130
the succession.

00:24:31.130 --> 00:24:35.870
Their plan was to formally reveal the Bourbon
prince as future king in January 1969, but

00:24:35.870 --> 00:24:40.930
this was delayed due to a series of terrorist
attacks from ETA, the Basque separatist.

00:24:40.930 --> 00:24:44.630
Finally in July Franco formally announced
that Juan Carlos of Bourbon would succeed

00:24:44.630 --> 00:24:46.360
him as head of State and King of Spain.

00:24:46.360 --> 00:24:50.820
In a televised ceremony, the prince swore
an oath of loyalty to the Caudillo and to

00:24:50.820 --> 00:24:52.030
the Nationalist Movement.

00:24:52.030 --> 00:24:57.841
By 1973 Franco was 81 and not able to handle
the day to day administration of the Government.

00:24:57.841 --> 00:25:01.360
He appointed Carrero Blanco as his Prime Minister,
maintaining an active role in some foreign

00:25:01.360 --> 00:25:02.570
policy matters.

00:25:02.570 --> 00:25:06.100
Carrero Blanco’s office was short lived:
on the 20th of December his car was blown

00:25:06.100 --> 00:25:11.880
up by ETA, in an explosion so violent that
the car flew over a church and landed on a

00:25:11.880 --> 00:25:12.880
2nd floor terrace.

00:25:12.880 --> 00:25:15.020
A macabre joke started to circulate in Spain:
‘Arriba España, Arriba Franco … más

00:25:15.020 --> 00:25:16.020
alto que Carrero Blanco!’

00:25:16.020 --> 00:25:17.020
Arriba meaning ‘long live’ but also ‘up
with’.

00:25:17.020 --> 00:25:19.090
So, it can be translated as
‘Up with Spain, Up with Franco … even

00:25:19.090 --> 00:25:21.450
higher than Carrero Blanco!”

00:25:21.450 --> 00:25:26.140
The subsequent crackdown on ETA led to five
terrorists being sentenced to death and executed

00:25:26.140 --> 00:25:28.210
in September of 1975.

00:25:28.210 --> 00:25:33.240
These death penalties caused massive demonstrations
in Madrid, Lisbon and the Basque city of San

00:25:33.240 --> 00:25:36.120
Sebastian, as well as international condemnation.

00:25:36.120 --> 00:25:39.560
Franco had firmly backed these, and future
executions.

00:25:39.560 --> 00:25:43.470
The dissent, the demonstrations, even the
jokes … all these were clear signs that

00:25:43.470 --> 00:25:48.530
times had definitely changed since the totalitarian
days of the 1940s and 1950s.

00:25:48.530 --> 00:25:54.250
The regime, regardless of the Caudillo’s
age, was nearing its end.

00:25:54.250 --> 00:26:01.150
Last Words
Generalisimo Francisco Franco Bahamonde had

00:26:01.150 --> 00:26:05.320
been a reluctant leader who had gained control
on the back of his brother’s schemes.

00:26:05.320 --> 00:26:10.360
He had been a nostalgic of the Empire, ready
to bargain his country’s entry in WWII to

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:11.730
regain North Africa.

00:26:11.730 --> 00:26:15.970
He had been the ruthless Caudillo who could
not tolerate opposition.

00:26:15.970 --> 00:26:21.550
And finally, the astute politician who had
sought to bring stability to a divided nation.

00:26:21.550 --> 00:26:26.080
Francisco Franco died on the 20th November
of 1975, of natural causes.

00:26:26.080 --> 00:26:30.700
His last words were reported as
“I ask pardon of all my enemies, as I pardon

00:26:30.700 --> 00:26:36.580
with all my heart all those who declared themselves
my enemy, although I did not consider them

00:26:36.580 --> 00:26:39.450
to be so”
In his last conversation with Prime Minister

00:26:39.450 --> 00:26:43.530
Navarro, he asked for the Spanish people to
rally around their new King Juan Carlos and

00:26:43.530 --> 00:26:46.430
‘to keep the lands of Spain united’.

00:26:46.430 --> 00:26:50.450
The Spanish did give their support to Juan
Carlos, who successfully led the transition

00:26:50.450 --> 00:26:51.450
to a full democracy.

00:26:51.450 --> 00:26:56.760
I am aware that Francisco Franco and the Civil
War remain a divisive topic to this day in

00:26:56.760 --> 00:27:00.270
Spain, so before I leave I will kindly ask
you not to turn the comments section into

00:27:00.270 --> 00:27:01.350
a rhetoric battle.

00:27:01.350 --> 00:27:05.530
Why don’t you use the comments instead to
suggest another character from the Civil War,

00:27:05.530 --> 00:27:07.330
maybe on the Republican side?

00:27:07.330 --> 00:27:23.510
That’s it from me, I hope you enjoyed this
video and as usual … thank you for watching!

